 ## Overview
-Global-Local Depth Normalization is a depth regularization technique for 3D Gaussian Splatting that prevents scale-invariant losses from overlooking minor depth variations, thereby improving detailed geometry reshaping in texture-rich regions [S1]. It sets itself apart from standard scale-invariant losses by combining global scale preservation with local scale amplification, addressing the issue where existing methods align depth maps to a fixed global scale and ignore small local errors [S1].
+Global-Local Depth Normalization is a depth regularization technique for 3D Gaussian Splatting that prevents scale-invariant losses from overlooking minor depth variations, thereby improving detailed geometry reshaping in texture-rich regions [S1]. It sets itself apart from standard scale-invariant losses by combining global scale preservation with local scale amplification, addressing the issue where existing methods align depth maps to a fixed global scale and ignore small local errors [S1]. It builds on [Hard and Soft Depth Regularization](9178-hard-and-soft-depth-regularization.md) and differs from siblings [Depth Reinitialization](9233-depth-reinitialization.md), [Position Regularization](11049-position-regularization.md), and [Coherent 3D Gaussian Optimization](14045-coherent-3d-gaussian-optimization.md) by explicitly decoupling absolute scale maintenance from local error amplification during gradient computation [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** Rendered depth map $\hat{D}$, ground truth depth map $D_{gt}$ (if available) or reference depth, and optimization hyperparameters.
-*   **Outputs:** Gradient updates for 3D Gaussian parameters (centers, scales, opacities) guided by a combined global-local depth loss.
+*   **Inputs:** Rendered depth map $\hat{D}$, ground truth depth map $D_{gt}$ (if available) or reference depth, and optimization hyperparameters [S1, G].
